(874817, 96948, 3080, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/>Hi there.  This definately meets the challenge in my opinion.  parallel lines going off into the distance definately says vanishing point to me.  I like the colors.  The greens are nice and strong, and I like how the red of the brick pillar stands out among the greens.<br/>The telephone pole thingy (as you have heard) is a distraction.  Stands out in the crowd, and doesn''t seem to really ''belong'' in the photo.<br/>Your focus and clarity on the fence is good.<br/>Lighting seems to be alright, no distracting shadows.  My only complaint there would be that the whitness of the lion thing is a bit too white, and therefor washes out some of the detail.<br/>I like the angle and framing you chose to do this at.  I like how it draws your eyes from left to right across the photo.  I also like that the fence is in the lower half of the photo, not dead center of the photo.<br/>As I look even closer, there is a bright red triangle in the lower right hand corner of the photo.  It''s really bright and stands out a lot after I notice it.  Is that something on the ground, or is this something you camera put there?  Seems really weird.<br/>Anyway, I think it''s a nice shot, the subject doesn''t make me jump out of my seat with excitement, but technically pretty well done.<br/>~Heather~</td>', '*Critique Club*Hi there.  This definately meets the challenge in my opinion.  parallel lines going off into the distance definately says vanishing point to me.  I like the colors.  The greens are nice and strong, and I like how the red of the brick pillar stands out among the greens.The telephone pole thingy (as you have heard) is a distraction.  Stands out in the crowd, and doesn''t seem to really ''belong'' in the photo.Your focus and clarity on the fence is good.Lighting seems to be alright, no distracting shadows.  My only complaint there would be that the whitness of the lion thing is a bit too white, and therefor washes out some of the detail.I like the angle and framing you chose to do this at.  I like how it draws your eyes from left to right across the photo.  I also like that the fence is in the lower half of the photo, not dead center of the photo.As I look even closer, there is a bright red triangle in the lower right hand corner of the photo.  It''s really bright and stands out a lot after I notice it.  Is that something on the ground, or is this something you camera put there?  Seems really weird.Anyway, I think it''s a nice shot, the subject doesn''t make me jump out of my seat with excitement, but technically pretty well done.~Heather~', '2004-08-24 20:20:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(874825, 96869, 3080, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/>Hi there.  The first thing I notice about this shot is the lighting and color.  I''m thinking the 2 go hand in hand here.  Seems unnaturally orange/yellow ish.  Unlike some of the people, I like the sidewalk in the front.  I think that''s a nice angle, and definatley portrays the vanishing point here.<br/>There is nothing about the shot that makes me jump out of my seat with excitement, however, it is a well composed shot in my opinion.  I''m thinking that I might have cropped the bright lights out of the shot.  Maybe cropped right below the bottom light.  I think that crop works for me.  I wonder how this would be if taken during the day.  I''m not thinking that the night shot is really adding to this shot for me.<br/>I think that the post at the very end of the sidewalk is interesting.  Kind of shows how far away into the distance your ''vanishing point'' can go.<br/>Overall, I think it could be more interesting with a slightly different crop, and taken during the daytime.  ~Heather~<br/></td>', '*Critique Club*Hi there.  The first thing I notice about this shot is the lighting and color.  I''m thinking the 2 go hand in hand here.  Seems unnaturally orange/yellow ish.  Unlike some of the people, I like the sidewalk in the front.  I think that''s a nice angle, and definatley portrays the vanishing point here.There is nothing about the shot that makes me jump out of my seat with excitement, however, it is a well composed shot in my opinion.  I''m thinking that I might have cropped the bright lights out of the shot.  Maybe cropped right below the bottom light.  I think that crop works for me.  I wonder how this would be if taken during the day.  I''m not thinking that the night shot is really adding to this shot for me.I think that the post at the very end of the sidewalk is interesting.  Kind of shows how far away into the distance your ''vanishing point'' can go.Overall, I think it could be more interesting with a slightly different crop, and taken during the daytime.  ~Heather~', '2004-08-24 20:32:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(874863, 96222, 4493, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Tara:</b></div><hr/><i> Danny, could you please tell us what lens you used for this shot, and what the focal length was when you shot the photo? The depth of field is unbelievable. <br/>Thank you.<br/> <br/>Tara </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>You''ll be even more surprised to hear that this was taken from a bus window. The driver pulled over so we could take a quick shot. I handheld a graduated neutral density filter over the lens. Here is some of the exif information from the shot.<br/> <br/>Nikon D100 <br/>Image Size: Large (3008 x 2000) <br/>Lens: 50-500mm F/4-6.3 D (sigma)<br/>Focal Length: 50mm <br/>Exposure Mode: Aperture <br/>Metering Mode: Multi-Pattern <br/>1/250 sec - F/16 <br/>Exposure Comp.: -0.7 EV <br/>Sensitivity: ISO 400 <br/>White Balance: Auto <br/>AF Mode: Manual <br/> <br/>-danny</td>', 'Originally posted by Tara: Danny, could you please tell us what lens you used for this shot, and what the focal length was when you shot the photo? The depth of field is unbelievable. Thank you. Tara   You''ll be even more surprised to hear that this was taken from a bus window. The driver pulled over so we could take a quick shot. I handheld a graduated neutral density filter over the lens. Here is some of the exif information from the shot. Nikon D100 Image Size: Large (3008 x 2000) Lens: 50-500mm F/4-6.3 D (sigma)Focal Length: 50mm Exposure Mode: Aperture Metering Mode: Multi-Pattern 1/250 sec - F/16 Exposure Comp.: -0.7 EV Sensitivity: ISO 400 White Balance: Auto AF Mode: Manual  -danny', '2004-08-24 20:58:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(875038, 96977, 19780, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>I think this was a highly original entry for this challenge. <br/> <br/>I like the choice of a street with a slight uphill - it aids in the vanishing point view. <br/> <br/>The shadows on the trees and firehydrant help to push they eye up the street to the vanishing point. <br/> <br/>The point of view seems just slightly off to me for the overall composition - it might need to be cropped a little along the right edge to have it conform a little more to the rule of thirds.<br/> <br/>I enjoy the choice of B&amp;W and found the exposure wonderfully moody.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club I think this was a highly original entry for this challenge.  I like the choice of a street with a slight uphill - it aids in the vanishing point view.  The shadows on the trees and firehydrant help to push they eye up the street to the vanishing point.  The point of view seems just slightly off to me for the overall composition - it might need to be cropped a little along the right edge to have it conform a little more to the rule of thirds. I enjoy the choice of B&W and found the exposure wonderfully moody.', '2004-08-24 22:09:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(875162, 96910, 12611, '<td valign="top">beautiful yellow green adn great grain in this one mona.</td>', 'beautiful yellow green adn great grain in this one mona.', '2004-08-24 23:08:50.000000', NULL, 0),
(875347, 96222, 3102, '<td valign="top">I have to admit that I''m bummed that it''s Tuesday night, because that means that this shot will soon be disappearing from the home page!  Congrats again on an awe-inspiring shot.</td>', 'I have to admit that I''m bummed that it''s Tuesday night, because that means that this shot will soon be disappearing from the home page!  Congrats again on an awe-inspiring shot.', '2004-08-25 00:13:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(875435, 96801, 22734, '<td valign="top">from the critique club:<br/>Being that is more a casual shot we will treat it as such. You certainly met the challenge and the idea to include a familiar father and daughter gives the image a human interest. The few minor things to improve the comp as is is to crop it tighter, right at the start of the visible rail. Now, the focus appears to be with the wooden planks because the image begins to blur rather rapidly. This could have been improved by closing the aperture one stop. You would then be shooting at 60th of a second but enought to stop the action since the figures are moving not across, but away from the camera.<br/> <br/>When you think of a vanishing point, then you want a deep dof and if the lighting at this times was not favorable, you should consider a brighter day or time of day.<br/> <br/>Blackdog hit the nail on the head regarding the distraction of the girls head. All movement should be towards the vanishing point. The side glance pulls the eye away.<br/> <br/>However, as is, the image is a charming capture of a walk in the park. You know, the challenge places limitations.</td>', 'from the critique club:Being that is more a casual shot we will treat it as such. You certainly met the challenge and the idea to include a familiar father and daughter gives the image a human interest. The few minor things to improve the comp as is is to crop it tighter, right at the start of the visible rail. Now, the focus appears to be with the wooden planks because the image begins to blur rather rapidly. This could have been improved by closing the aperture one stop. You would then be shooting at 60th of a second but enought to stop the action since the figures are moving not across, but away from the camera. When you think of a vanishing point, then you want a deep dof and if the lighting at this times was not favorable, you should consider a brighter day or time of day. Blackdog hit the nail on the head regarding the distraction of the girls head. All movement should be towards the vanishing point. The side glance pulls the eye away. However, as is, the image is a charming capture of a walk in the park. You know, the challenge places limitations.', '2004-08-25 00:49:35.000000', NULL, 0),
(877854, 96748, 1031, '<td valign="top">Well, I beat you (barely) in overall placement, but I see you got three 10''s to only one for me : )<br/> <br/>I think this was somewhat under-rated (surprise!), but of course most people don''t want to deal with things like literally vanishing lines which don''t happen to converge in the image ...</td>', 'Well, I beat you (barely) in overall placement, but I see you got three 10''s to only one for me : ) I think this was somewhat under-rated (surprise!), but of course most people don''t want to deal with things like literally vanishing lines which don''t happen to converge in the image ...', '2004-08-25 23:18:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(878410, 96846, 26000, '<td valign="top">Damn.<br/>This is really good!</td>', 'Damn.This is really good!', '2004-08-26 11:16:21.000000', NULL, 0),
(884333, 96758, 4226, '<td valign="top">hey Lita,<br/>I like this picture, the angle that you chose and the texture and clarity. But this needs a little more DOF IMO, also give a little more space for the lines to merge a little ( the crop it too tight ). I don''t know if F8 is the min. aperture your camera supports, but this might look better with a lesser aperture and if you showed the expanse more.<br/>Also try to focus on nearly 1/3rd distance away from you keeping a low aperture, that might help increase your overall depth of field.<br/>You can also try to convert this to b/w using channel mixer and get a more vibrant picture. the textures might get more prominent with a b/w conversion.<br/>cheers,<br/>KG<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2004-08-30 16:54:17.</i></td>', 'hey Lita,I like this picture, the angle that you chose and the texture and clarity. But this needs a little more DOF IMO, also give a little more space for the lines to merge a little ( the crop it too tight ). I don''t know if F8 is the min. aperture your camera supports, but this might look better with a lesser aperture and if you showed the expanse more.Also try to focus on nearly 1/3rd distance away from you keeping a low aperture, that might help increase your overall depth of field.You can also try to convert this to b/w using channel mixer and get a more vibrant picture. the textures might get more prominent with a b/w conversion.cheers,KG', '2004-08-30 17:53:27.000000', '2004-08-30 17:54:17.000000', 0),
(895962, 96500, 20066, '<td valign="top">Vá, frábær mynd af Vatnsendahæðinni.  Hefði átt að fá meira en 5,6 í einkunn.</td>', 'Vá, frábær mynd af Vatnsendahæðinni.  Hefði átt að fá meira en 5,6 í einkunn.', '2004-09-06 16:18:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(896294, 96205, 15242, '<td valign="top">Had you walked up the tracks about 100 yards you could probably make most of the clutter go away.  Also, the discoloring on the bottom of the picture (the road?) is distracting.</td>', 'Had you walked up the tracks about 100 yards you could probably make most of the clutter go away.  Also, the discoloring on the bottom of the picture (the road?) is distracting.', '2004-09-06 19:52:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(896620, 96763, 10084, '<td valign="top">Not sure if you have a circular polarize filter but I can see some extreme potential to up the blues with it to really make those sweeping clouds pop out of the shot.<br/> <br/>I kind of like the fence post in the shot but it takes away from the theme of the challenge. Your eyes automatically focus in on the post because of the contrast of the sky, trees, etc. If it wasn''t included the viewers eyes would follow the lines of the trees,shrub,etc.<br/> <br/>Eye leading is what makes a shot interesting and memorable. I know that I myself need some help in this :)</td>', 'Not sure if you have a circular polarize filter but I can see some extreme potential to up the blues with it to really make those sweeping clouds pop out of the shot. I kind of like the fence post in the shot but it takes away from the theme of the challenge. Your eyes automatically focus in on the post because of the contrast of the sky, trees, etc. If it wasn''t included the viewers eyes would follow the lines of the trees,shrub,etc. Eye leading is what makes a shot interesting and memorable. I know that I myself need some help in this :)', '2004-09-06 23:48:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(906087, 96232, 26743, '<td valign="top">good picture, i did the exact same thing but i used more candles and shot it in black and white, it''s funny when you first discover it it''s such a rush. Mirror are just so facinating</td>', 'good picture, i did the exact same thing but i used more candles and shot it in black and white, it''s funny when you first discover it it''s such a rush. Mirror are just so facinating', '2004-09-12 18:07:21.000000', NULL, 0),
(913721, 96751, 24896, '<td valign="top">I hope you didn''t lose your shirt on this one...LOL your comment says " I''m putting my money on this being my highest scoring sho at dpc :"<br/> <br/>Well you kicked my butt...I tried to stay away from the obvious VP too but it got trashed.  The blue is a little over processed as you already know from the other comments. I think you composition is a little too centered the triangular shapes to the right are distracting. Positioning your shot a third to the right would eliminated these shapes bringing the blue lines from the left into focal points (rule of thirds)and also allowing for more detail from the clouds in the sky. But a great subject none the less.<br/></td>', 'I hope you didn''t lose your shirt on this one...LOL your comment says " I''m putting my money on this being my highest scoring sho at dpc :" Well you kicked my butt...I tried to stay away from the obvious VP too but it got trashed.  The blue is a little over processed as you already know from the other comments. I think you composition is a little too centered the triangular shapes to the right are distracting. Positioning your shot a third to the right would eliminated these shapes bringing the blue lines from the left into focal points (rule of thirds)and also allowing for more detail from the clouds in the sky. But a great subject none the less.', '2004-09-16 21:38:53.000000', NULL, 0),
